{"type": "message", "message": "**Minimalist means keeping what makes your home work—and reducing visual clutter, not comfort.** For a small flat, that usually means:\n\n- **A few well-chosen pieces:** furniture sized for the room, rather than lots of small extras.\n- **Clear routes:** arrange furniture so moving between rooms feels easy.\n- **Useful storage:** closed cupboards keep everyday necessities handy but out of sight.\n- **A restrained palette:** a few complementary colours and materials, with wood or textiles adding warmth.\n- **Personal touches with breathing room:** favourite books, art or objects—not bare surfaces everywhere.\n\nFor your combined living-and-dining room, the principle would be two clear, coordinated zones rather than furniture scattered throughout. Minimalism should still provide comfortable seating, a useful table and good lighting—not simply the maximum empty floor.\n\nI can discuss your flat, but cannot check or change its layout until its geometry is supported.", "conversationId": "92e1fa01fecf4499b3661b67f4b26b50"}
